\par\par \b \uc2  FUND VIEW-Caution urged on Jakarta  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  board. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sarah Davison \par \uc2 656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Februar 1998\par 03:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Feb 15 (Reuters) - Asian fund managers have expressed a high degree of scepticism over proposals by Indonesia to introduce a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board mechanism. \par \par \uc2  They said Indonesia needed confidence rather than a new monetary system to shore up the rupiah, now trading at 8,000 to the U.S. dollar after hitting a record low of 17,000 last month. \par \par \uc2  "Re-establishing confidence is of critical importance," said Marshall Mays, chief strategist at Nikko Securities. \par \par \uc2  Tough action to clean up rotten banks, proof that nepotism and corruption were things of the past and concrete steps to solve Indonesia's huge private sector debt problem were the only possible routes to a return in confidence, fund managers said. \par \par \uc2  "The real issue in Indonesia is how do we really progress, move forward on these issues, and I don't think they can be solved just by a different exchange rate mechanism," said Eugene Chung, chief Asian investment strategist at SBC Warburg. \par \par \uc2  Politics remained the key, the strategists said. \par \par \uc2  President Suharto appears to be backing a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board as a quicker method of raising the value of the rupiah \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a bid to bring down prices and help resolve the debt issue. \par \par \uc2  Some critics say vested interests in Indonesia see a pegged rupiah as a way of protecting their assets. \par \par \uc2  "It's hugely popular with the crowd in charge right now because they have a hope they can hold onto their goodies, where if they have to give up monopolies, do the right thing and restructure the system, they would suffer," said Mays. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board would fix the rupiah to an external \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the U.S. dollar or Japanese yen, with full cash-backing for the amount of rupiah in circulation. \par \par \uc2  In theory, the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would then forfeit control over monetary policy to the United States or Japan, implying lower rates along with a stable rupiah. \par \par \uc2  In practice, Asia's experience with fixed exchange rates has demonstrated investors' insistence on country risk premiums over and above the rates applied to the external \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "In Hong Kong, we know from experience that interest rates are a function of the risk premium," said Chung. \par \par \uc2  Those premiums made a substantial contribution to the Asian crisis. Huge and volatile U.S. dollar inflows seeking high interest and no \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk ramped inflation throughout Asia, swelling credit and building unsustainable asset price bubbles. \par \par \uc2  Those asset bubbles collapsed like dominoes after Thailand devalued the baht last July 2, sparking rapid capital flight. \par \par \uc2  If Indonesia fixes its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, those inflows could return and repeat a process clearly identified as dangerous. \par \par \uc2  "The risk that people will borrow U.S. dollars again, assuming its guaranteed, is not inconsiderable," said Mays. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors were also sceptical of Indonesia's political will to allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board to work independently -- and independence would be key to its successful \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We're not even sure we have that political will here in Hong Kong," said Chung. "In theory, things can work out beautifully but in practice, politics does matter unfortunately. Politics is everything in Asia." \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong has faced criticism for acting to ease the interest rate burden during speculative attacks on the Hong Kong dollar, pegged at a rate of HK$7.80 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Although most commentators supported the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , others argued the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board should be allowed to work in total isolation from interference, with rates moving only according to market demand for the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The fear among foreign investors is that Indonesia will refuse to abide by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board when rates shoot high enough to damage corporate activity. \par \par \uc2  Instead of allowing higher rates to force economic adjustment -- bankruptcies or bank failures -- Indonesia could instead resort to printing money to maintain the status quo, the fund managers said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926du2f03y46\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
