\par\par \b \uc2  HK Dollar Weaker Late; Pulled Down By U.S., China Accounts \b0\par\par\uc2 308 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 1998\par 10:25\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong dollar was weaker in late trading Thursday from levels a day ago, thrown off intraday highs reached in London and New York on U.S. dollar buying by U.S. and Chinese accounts. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said mainland banks were buying to get cheap U.S. dollars, while U.S. accounts may be trying to sell the Hong Kong dollars short. \par \par \uc2  Around 0906 GMT (4:06 a.m. EST), the U.S. dollar is quoted at HK$7.7460, up from HK$7.7360 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  'There were some orders which is why the TT (U.S. dollar against Hong Kong dollar) went lower last night,' a treasury chief at a foreign bank said. 'But I believe there's some speculators that are trying to go long the U.S. dollar so that's why the TT is going back up.' \par \par \uc2  In overnight trading, the Hong Kong dollar strengthened to HK$7.7300 to the U.S. dollar at one point on a combination of large U.S. dollar sell orders and possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  One U.S. dollar sell order believed to be around US$2.0-3.0 billion is said to have forced the Hong Kong dollar to its global intraday high right when the London market opened Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  However, local television reports in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the HKMA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has been hovering near the psychologically-important HK$7.7500 to the U.S. dollar level for several weeks. \par \par \uc2  An HKMA source, however, said the quasi-\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has kept a 'passive' stance in the markets, acting only as a counterparty to those who want to cover short positions. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 08-01-98 \par \par \uc2  0925GMT \par \par \uc2  1053 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du18005hj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
