\par\par \b \uc2  Hong Kong dlr seen under pressure on Asian fears. \b0\par\par\uc2 559 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Dec 12 (Reuters) - Renewed fear of further dives in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were likely to fuel more selling of the already weakened Hong Kong dollar, dealers said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The Hong Kong dollar will certainly march through the key HK$7.75 as New York and London start trading," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht hit an all-time low of 45.3 per U.S dollar on Friday, after the South Korean won rocked the regional market. \par \par \uc2  So far at least three U.S investment banks were heard short selling the Hong Kong dollar in the forward market this week, but dealers said there was more selling seen from Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "Selling on the Hong Kong dollar came mainly from overseas players, that is for sure," a dealer noted. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar weakened by about one half Hong Kong cent  to close at HK$7.7490/HK$7.7510 versus HK$7.7450/60 at Thursday's close. The local unit opened softer at HK$7.7470/80 before repeatedly testing the key 7.75. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar has dropped by a slight 0.18 percent, compared with the South Korean won's dramatic drop of some 25 percent since Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were repeated and consistent offers at 7.75 and a couple of small transactions traded at the 7.75 level. \par \par \uc2  There were rumours the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, buying \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars for U.S. dollars, but this could not be confirmed. \par \par \uc2  The HK$7.75 level is thought by dealers to be closely watched by the HKMA. \par \par \uc2  As far as the local money and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Friday was concerned, trading was thinner than Thursday as market players basically squared positions towards the weekend. \par \par \uc2  "After all, you don't know what will happen when New York or London starts trading," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The three-month dollar forward eased slightly to 950-1,050 points from 1,050-1,150 at midday, 1,150-1,250 in early trade and 1,000-1,100 at Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2  The six-month swap was a hair lower at 2,300-2,500 points from 2,500-2,650 points at midday, 2,600-2,650 in early trade and 2,400-2,600 points at Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2  In a reversal of its tighter policy in October, the HKMA was seen offering more longer-dated three-month straight money into the market, though in very small quantities. \par \par \uc2  "They (the HKMA) if just trying to tell everybody that they are not afraid of another speculative attack," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The three-month Hong Kong dollar deposit rate eased modestly to 10.7-11 percent from 11.5-12.50 in early trade and Thursday's close. The six-month was lower at 11-12.50 percent from 12-13 percent in early trade and Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2  "The market will react as long as HKMA make a moves, it doesn't really matter how much they offer," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong Financial Secretary Donald Tsang on Friday said there was no evidence of speculative pressure on the Hong Kong dollar this week. \par \par \uc2  --Hong Kong Newsroom 852-2847-4039; Fax 852-2845-0636 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtcc017co\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
