\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-HK stocks slide in morning, dollar worries return. \b0\par\par\uc2 257 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 1997\par 05:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Dec 11 (Reuters) - Regional worries dealt a heavy blow to Hong Kong stocks on Thursday with the Hang Seng Index sliding more than five percent at one stage in early trade as a freefall in the Korean won put pressure on the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Index was down 546 points, or 4.96 percent, at 10,475 in morning trade after hitting a low of 10,411 earlier. \par \par \uc2  Turnover was HK$4.32 billion after the first hour of trade. \par \par \uc2  "People are worried about the Hong Kong dollar and the Korean crisis and where the bottom is going to be. Hong Kong cannot be completely isolated from this," said Geoff Galbraith, vice president at DBS Securities. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won continued its freefall on Thursday, plunging to its lower limit and a historic low of 1,719.8 against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Local interbank rates pushed higher as the slide in the Korean won caused the Hong Kong dollar to weaken. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at HK$7.7486/96 to the U.S. dollar and was expected to test the key level of HK$7.75. \par \par \uc2  "As the Hong kong dollar has fallen close to the warning level of HK$7.75 to the U.S. dollar, there is speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  by pushing up the interbank rate," said one sales manager at a regional brokerage. (HK$7.73 = US$1) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtcb0158q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
