\par\par \b \uc2  No won-linked speculation on HK dlr - HKMA's Yam. \b0\par\par\uc2 617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Nov 19 (Reuters) - Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) chief Joseph Yam said on Wednesday the territory's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had detected no Hong Kong dollar speculation linked to the slide in the South Korean won. \par \par \uc2  "I haven't heard or seen anything that suggests people are borrowing quite a lot of Hong Kong dollars so they can take advantage of a particular event to short the Hong Kong dollar," Yam told reporters at a briefing. \par \par \uc2  Although the overnight rate has returned to about five percent from 300 percent during attacks on the Hong Kong dollar in October, long-term rates remain high, reflecting an increased Asian risk premium following contagious \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation, Yam said. \par \par \uc2  "I expect these rates to come down further but if there is yet another event in the region, for example in Korea, then sentiment may turn again and banks may become more cautious," he said. \par \par \uc2  The recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  attack started in earnest on October 21 with massive selling of Hong Kong dollars for U.S. dollars, Yam said. Selling of Hong Kong dollars increased on October 22. \par \par \uc2  But on October 23, now known as Black Thursday due to the largest one-day point fall in the history of the blue-chip Hang Seng Index, Hong Kong banks were required to give the HKMA nearly HK$5 billion to settle their U.S. dollar transactions. \par \par \uc2  This left less than HK$1 billion in the banking system, Yam said. \par \par \uc2  "On Thursday (October 23), the net amount of balance, Hong Kong dollar balance in all accounts in all banks in Hong Kong was only about less than HK$1 billion, about HK$500 million or so," he said. \par \par \uc2  According to Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system requiring full U.S.-dollar backing for all Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in circulation, Hong Kong banks were left without local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to settle Hong Kong-U.S. dollar trades. \par \par \uc2  This persuaded Hong Kong banks to bid up the overnight rate to 300 percent in a mad scramble for Hong Kong dollars, quickly forcing banks back to the HKMA to sell U.S. dollars to raise Hong Kong dollars to settle their earlier transactions. \par \par \uc2  Yam said the overnight rate might have gone as high as 1,000 percent had the HKMA not opted to lend banks Hong Kong dollars in advance of U.S. dollar transactions. \par \par \uc2  "We made exceptions and we actually did these \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  deals for value the same day," Yam said. \par \par \uc2  "I also...started lending the money to them in order to relieve them for the weekend, in other words, creating Hong Kong dollars and lending to them over the weekend. In advance of settlement, I was actually providing money to the market." \par \par \uc2  This "discretion" earned the HKMA some criticism for interfering in the "auto-pilot" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board mechanism and accusations that it squeezed rates so hard it added momentum to the Hong Kong stock market fall. \par \par \uc2  "We did not exercise that much discretion," Yam said. \par \par \uc2  "We are the lender of last resort," he added. "And if I had not done anything, the interest rates could have gone up to maybe 1,000 percent." \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves at the end of October increased rather than declined as a result of HKMA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the attack, Yam said. \par \par \uc2  The end of October figures have yet to be released. \par \par \uc2  Yam, who stressed repeatedly Hong Kong's commitment to maintaining the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  link to the U.S. dollar, also said the HKMA did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward market. \par \par \uc2  -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  Newsroom (852) 2843 6470; Fax (852) 2845 0636 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbj02eng\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
