\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  uninspired as dollar drops. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 994 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 1997\par 11:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Nov 13 (Reuters) - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  softened on Thursday while the Indonesian rupiah found itself torn between its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and companies seeking dollars. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said IMF Managing Director Michel Camdessus's calming words about Southeast Asia's efforts to pull out of months of economic turmoil were largely ignored by the market. \par \par \uc2  Camdessus, on a tour of Southeast Asia, said he believed Asia would quickly rebound from its current crisis and should be able to return to high rates of economic growth. \par \par \uc2  He said Thailand had passed its first IMF review while Hong Kong and Malaysian banks were unlikely to face a crisis. \par \par \uc2  He also said Malaysia was in solid economic condition, though its current account gap was still on "the higher side". \par \par \uc2  In Indonesia, the rupiah was pushed to a high of 3,355 to the dollar in early trade on talk Bank Indonesia had sold dollars above the 3,400 level in Jakarta and Singapore markets. \par \par \uc2  But there was no official confirmation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and no clear sign the central banks of Japan and Singapore were involved in the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as was the case twice last week. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah drifted lower on dollar demand from offshore parties, especially Japanese companies, in afternoon trade. It was quoted at 3,400/10 to the dollar at 1010 GMT amid vague talk Bank Indonesia had checked rates above the 3,400 level again. \par \par \uc2  "We heard BI had been checking rates for the third time but I'm not sure if they actually sold dollars," a U.S. bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think BI coming in alone would scare the market. It's unlikely they'll have that much effect unless they come in with the Bank of Japan (BOJ) and \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS)," she added. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese finance ministry and the MAS declined to comment on whether the BOJ had taken part in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  looked soft but dealers said the market lacked energy as many players were winding down for the end of the year and the dollar's abrupt drop against the yen on talk of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also dominated interest. \par \par \uc2  In Thailand, the baht was steady but weak at 38.70/80 to the dollar against 38.55/65 late on Wednesday. The offshore rate was at 38.30/31 against 37.999/00. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was awaiting the announcement of Thai Prime Minister Chuan Leekpai's new cabinet. Chuan was due to present his cabinet list for approval to King Bhumibol Adulyadej later on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the baht did not react to comments by the IMF's Camdessus that the Fund had no plans to make changes to Thailand's economic programme. \par \par \uc2  In Bangkok, Supachai Panitchpakdi, expected to be named deputy premier in charge of economic affairs in the new cabinet, appeared to echo the IMF chief's statements. \par \par \uc2  He said Thailand did not intend to renegotiate the terms of the IMF-led $17.2 billion economic bailout package. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar eased to 1.5795/10 to the U.S. dollar against 1.5746/66 on Wednesday on buying of the Malaysian ringgit/Singapore dollar cross. \par \par \uc2  "There's been some ringgit/Sing buying going through. There's talk that the cross might go to 50 cents but I think it has to break 48.20 first," a European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit was at 47.70/89 Singapore cents at 1010 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Across the border, the ringgit showed little sign of stirring out of the range it has established against the U.S. dollar in recent sessions, hovering around 3.3000/100. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso ended lower at 33.93 per dollar against a previous close of 33.43 on dollar short-covering and offshore dollar demand. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell about $4 million at 33.98 pesos in late trade, but further falls were expected to be capped by inflows from overseas contract workers. \par \par \uc2  In north Asia, the Korean won ended up at 986.70 to the dollar as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept to its staunch defence, selling dollars at 988.00 won in opening trade while repeating its daily words of support. \par \par \uc2  "Our stance to maintain the won steadily stronger has not changed," said Lee Eung-baek, chief of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  division. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Korea has moved its defence line up by one won to the dollar for each of the last two days, steering the embattled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  away from the 1,000 level eyed by the market. \par \par \uc2  But its actions have taken their toll on the money market where the overnight call loan rate hit a yearly high of 14.90 percent on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Seoul traders said the market was awaiting a government package of measures to stabilise financial markets. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar slid to T$31.105 against its previous T$30.992 close on jitters about the weak yen and won. \par \par \uc2  After the market close, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said there was no change in its monetary policy for the time being and it reserved the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when needed. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar ended lower at 7.7320/40 to the U.S. dollar from Wednesday's 7.7300/10 close as interest rates eased. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said liquidity improved after the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) clarified its rules on borrowing from its discount window. \par \par \uc2  On October 23, the HKMA had said it would levy penal rates on "repeated borrowers" at the window, making banks wary of tapping the facility and leading to a liquidity crunch. \par \par \uc2  The HKMA said on Thursday repeated borrowers are licensed banks which have borrowed through the facility on eight occasions in any period of 25 days or on four consecutive days in which the window is open. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbd02lxk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
