\par\par \b \uc2  Hong Kong to survive speculative attacks - Greenwood. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Angela Tan \par \uc2 499 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 1997\par 06:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 28 (Reuters) - Investors should not be too alarmist about the impact that recent speculative attacks on the Hong Kong dollar will have on the territory's economy, LGT Asset Management chief economist John Greenwood said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Greenwood, the architect of the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to the U.S. dollar, told a conference the Hong Kong economy was flexible enough to weather the fluctuations and the peg would survive the speculative attacks. \par \par \uc2  "I doubt Hong Kong will suffer the same downturn like Thailand," he said. \par \par \uc2  "The Hong Kong dollar is pegged by the means of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  boards are inherently stronger than \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegs," Greenwood said later in an interview with Reuters Financial Television (RFTV). \par \par \uc2  "Fundamentally, the Hong Kong authorities have reserves six or seven times greater than the issued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  notes," he said. \par \par \uc2  "More importantly, the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not have to use its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to protect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Under the current system, what happens is the whole economy adjusts to the exchange rate rather than the other way around," he said. \par \par \uc2  Asked if a sustained attack on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would hurt the economy, Greenwood said defending the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would mean pushing interest rates up, which would attract money from abroad. \par \par \uc2  "The rates would have to stay up for several months in order to do any real damage to the economy," Greenwood said. \par \par \uc2  "I don't believe that a few days or weeks of high rates would do any significant damage to the economy. The high rates are only in the inter-bank market and have not percolated to rates for mortgages," he added. \par \par \uc2  Greenwood dismissed suggestions the Hong Kong dollar had to be devalued for the economy to stay competitive against other Asian countries which saw their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values slashed drastically against the U.S. dollar in recent months. \par \par \uc2  Governments should put in place micro and macro policies and restore investors' confidence instead of adopting an \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  approach, he said. \par \par \uc2  Greenwood said he expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  market would continue to see further adjustments in equities and property prices as interest rates changed. \par \par \uc2  "However, typically these interest rates adjustments last for weeks, not months. I would expect the same kind of thing on this occasion. The only caveat to that scenario is if we have continuing problems in the ASEAN region which have knock-on effects on Asia, then that will affect Hong Kong," he said. \par \par \uc2  ASEAN, the Association of Southeast Asian Nations, groups Brunei, Burma, Indonesia, Laos, Malaysia, the Philippines, Singapore, Thailand and Vietnam. \par \par \uc2  Greenwood said he believed Wall Street's 7.18 percent plunge on Monday to 7,161.15 was a "short-term blow but not an end to a bull economy". \par \par \uc2  "It is a temporary event, a stock market event rather than an economic event," Greenwood said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. economic fundamentals would remain sound amid low inflationary pressure, he said. \par \par \uc2  (c