\par\par \b \uc2  China/HK Govt./Market -3: Reiterated Earlier Statements \b0\par\par\uc2 280 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 1997\par 09:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tang's comments Tuesday largely mirror statements made by Foreign\par Ministry spokesman Shen Guofang at last Thursday's regular press\par briefing.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par According to analysts, the sell-off in the Hong Kong stock market was\par triggered by concerns over the island's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and its 14-year peg to\par the U.S. dollar. As the Hong Kong dollar came under increasing pressure\par from international speculators, many market participants began to wonder\par if the mainland government would devote part of its $134 billion in\par foreign reserves to the defense of the island's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  An official with the central People's Bank of China, who spoke with Dow Jones Tuesday on condition of anonymity, said he had no knowledge of the People's Bank of China \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in any manner in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. He said the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  had both the capability and authority to handle the current situation on its own. \par \par \uc2  However, in an interview last Friday with a Hong Kong newspaper, People's Bank of China governor Dai Xianglong acknowledged China would aid Hong Kong in staving off attacks on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if 'necessary' or if requested by the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 . To date, both Hong Kong and mainland officials have denied such help has been requested or given. \par \par \uc2  The former U.K. colony reverted to mainland control in July and is now a Special Administrative Region of China. Under the banner of 'one country, two systems,' Hong Kong has been promised a large degree of freedom to manage its own affairs for at least the next 50 years. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 28-10-97 \par \par \uc2  0830GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dtas086hp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
