\par\par \b \uc2  HK$ Bonds Rally On Back Of U.S. Tsys, Flight To Quality \b0\par\par\uc2 374 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 1997\par 06:17\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Hong Kong government bonds\par rallied midday Tuesday in step with a rise in U.S. Treasurys\par and a growing preference by some investors to hold\par government debt in the face of the stock market sell-off,\par traders said.\par    One broker at a North American house said one U.S. and\par one European bank bought bills this morning, driving prices\par up in the short-term sector by 100 basis points. The price\par movement was exaggerated since liquidity was light, traders\par said.\par    Turnover was around HK$3.3 billion late Monday.\par    At 0455 GMT (11:55 p.m. EST)the 10-year Exchange Fund\par bond yield rose to 8.70%, compared with 11.30% late Monday.\par The one-week Exchange Fund bill yield rose to 7.89%,\par compared with 7.56%.\par    "We're up because of the U.S. Treasury market," a trader\par at an Asian bank said. "There was some buying activity right\par when the market opened; some banks want to hold safer\par investments."\par    U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Treasurys rose sharply in Tokyo as investors sought\par safer investments amid the Hong Kong and Japanese stock\par market falls.\par    So far, Hong Kong stocks have plunged 15.4% on the back\par of a steep sell-off in the U.S. and persistent concerns\par about high interest rates and the strength of the Hong Kong\par dollar.\par    The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is believed to have\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to shore up\par confidence over the stock market drop. The HKMA had no\par comment on its activities.\par    At 0500 GMT the U.S. dollar is quoted at HK$7.7220, down\par from HK$7.7395 around the same time Monday.\par    At the same time, the Hong Kong Interbank Offered Rate\par is quoted at 9%, down from 12%.\par    Under current market conditions, interbank rate spreads\par are extremely wide and quotations vary from bank to bank.\par    For more information on Hong Kong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par money market activity, bill and note prices and other\par related information, please refer to Dow Jones Markets page\par - Betty W. Liu; (852) 2802-7002; bwliu@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) DOW JONES NEWS  10-28-97\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0017EST\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  38700 4118 4178 4340 5096 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011006dtas02acr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
