\par\par \b \uc2  PRESS DIGEST - HK Newspaper Headlines - Oct 24. \b0\par\par\uc2 273 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Oct 24 (Reuters) - These are some of the leading stories in the Hong Kong press. Reuters has not verified these stories and does not vouch for their accuracy. \par \par \uc2  -- ECONOMIC JOURNAL - Hong Kong's stock market plunge triggered a global sell-off. Hong Kong officials stressed that Hong Kong dollar speculation had been defeated. \par \par \uc2  -- A Chinese government spokesman said China would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stock and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, because the stock market should operate according to market rules. \par \par \uc2  -- ECONOMIC TIMES - The Land Fund used a large amount of its reserves to buy Hong Kong dollars and leading blue chip shares yesterday. The investment decision was to show the Fund's confidence in the Hong Kong stock market. \par \par \uc2  -- The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced that Hong Kong's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves had reached $681 billion, the third biggest in the world, after Japan and mainland China. \par \par \uc2  -- APPLE DAILY - Since the stock market crisis, taxi and nightclub busines has dropped about 20 per cent, while a price war was predicted in the restaurant sector to attract business. \par \par \uc2  -- SOUTH CHINA MORNING POST - China Construction Bank president Wang Qishan criticised Morgan Stanley for encouraging investors to desert the Hong Kong stock market. \par \par \uc2  -- Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief executive Joseph Yam said he would exact a high price from speculators shorting the Hong Kong dollar, by charging a high penal rate. \par \par \uc2  -- HONGKONG STANDARD - Banks began restricting withdrawals of deposits as the fallout from the stock market crash and uncertainty over the dollar peg hit the financial system. \par \par \uc2  -- Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843 6441. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtao03uj5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
