\par\par \b \uc2  China  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  says would help Hong Kong. \b0\par\par\uc2 621 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 1997\par 07:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIJING, Oct 24 (Reuters) - China would respond if Hong Kong's monetary authorities asked for help, a Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said on Friday, commenting on the speculative assault on the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  "If anything happened to Hong Kong, and if Hong Kong monetary authorities requested us to do something, then the relevant departments would find concrete solutions," said an official in the spokesman's office of the People's Bank of China. \par \par \uc2  The official, who declined to be identified, was speaking in a telephone interview. \par \par \uc2  "When Hong Kong returned to China, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stressed that the mainland and Hong Kong would have two separate monetary authorities and Hong Kong affairs would be mainly handled by Hong Kong," he said. \par \par \uc2  "Under any circumstances, China would not use Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves," he said. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong was returned to China by colonial power Britain at the stroke of midnight on June 30 under a "one country, two systems" formula, which guaranteed the territory's capitalist way of life would remain unchanged for 50 years. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong stocks, pounded into the ground on Thursday, rose sharply on Friday and closed the morning session with the blue chip Hang Seng Index 470.75 points, or 4.52 percent, higher at 10,897.05, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  Overnight rates in Hong Kong came down to about 10 percent in early afternoon from a peak of 300 percent on Thursday, prompted by worries about the stability of the Hong Kong-U.S. dollar peg. \par \par \uc2  A Hong Kong newspaper on Friday ran an interview with People's Bank of China Governor Dai Xianglong in which he pledged support for the local dollar. \par \par \uc2  The interview was conducted by Peter Seidlitz, the Beijing-based correspondent with the German newspaper Handelsblatt. \par \par \uc2  Seidlitz said the interview took place on Wednesday, one day before the collapse of the Hong Kong stock market. \par \par \uc2  "I have full confidence in the stability of the Hong Kong dollar," Dai was quoted as saying. \par \par \uc2  "What is more important is that the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) has the competence to maintain its stability," Dai said. \par \par \uc2  "If it is necessary -- or at the request of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region Government or the HKMA -- the Chinese government will provide support to the Hong Kong dollar," he said. \par \par \uc2  "Besides, the International Monetary Fund will also give its support for Hong Kong's financial stability," he added. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  is Hong Kong's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesman said Dai's comments were "not directly connected to what was happening in Hong Kong". \par \par \uc2  Separately, an economist at a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  research institute said China would certainly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar if China believed its national interests were under threat. \par \par \uc2  "If they believe the situation over there is influencing the national reputation, and if it was necessary, we would definitely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said the economist at the Finance and Banking Research Institute. \par \par \uc2  "We have the strength to do that," said the economist, who declined to be identified. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar has been pegged to the U.S. dollar since 1983 at a rate of HK$7.80 to US$1. It is allowed to float in a narrow band, but normally trades on the strong side of the peg. \par \par \uc2  Chief Executive Tung Chee-hwa vowed to defend the peg after he returned to Hong Kong on Friday from Europe. \par \par \uc2  China announced on Thursday its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves hit $134.07 billion at the end of September, up $29.04 billion from the end of last year. Hong Kong says it has reserves of $88 billion. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtao03uae\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
