\par\par \b \uc2  WSJE: Financial Meltdown In HK Puts China To The Test \b0\par\par\uc2 684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 1997\par 05:11\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    From Friday's Wall Street Journal Europe\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--How long can \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  prop up its battered\par dollar? And will Beijing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ?\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  Those were the multibillion-dollar questions as a brutal attack on the territory's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted monetary authorities to ratchet up interest rates as high as 300% in a spirited defense that drove Hong Kong shares down 10.4%, the biggest drop in their history, and sent shock waves through financial markets around the globe. \par \par \uc2  The nerve-wracking free fall on the blue-chip Hang Seng Index jolted investors in Europe and the U.S., rudely reminding bull-market buyers that stock prices can fall as well as rise. By the time the dust settled Thursday night, stock sellers had lopped more than 3% off the value of shares traded in London, Frankfurt and Paris, while the Dow Jones Industrial Average dropped 2.33%. \par \par \uc2  But the biggest shock probably came in Beijing, where the debacle will sorely test the communist government's hands-off policy toward its new territory. \par \par \uc2  The meltdown is just the sort of crisis that Hong Kong long feared would draw Beijing's meddling, particularly since the catalyst in this case is essentially political: the decision to defend the Hong Kong dollar's link to the U.S. dollar rather than devalue the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  along with those in the rest of Southeast Asia. \par \par \uc2  China could, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders say, stop the speculation by stating that it will defend the Hong Kong dollar with the mainland's own reserves. Such a step would likely face stiff opposition, economists say, from Chinese leaders bent against spending mainland money to defend a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is arguably overvalued. But the consequences of not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  may prove too grave for China to ignore. \par \par \uc2  The quickening deflation of Hong Kong's bubble economy threatens to undermine the territory's competitiveness against Asian countries that have already devalued. It could also choke off new investments by companies; pummel the local real-estate market, where Beijing owns loads of property; and wreak havoc on China's recent decision to accelerate economic reforms on the mainland by floating shares in state companies. \par \par \uc2  Thus Beijing faces a brutal choice: "Asset prices or the peg," said Andy Xie, an economist at Morgan Stanley Dean Witter in Hong Kong. "You can only choose one, because - if you defend the exchange rate - you have to maintain high interest rates." \par \par \uc2  So far, Hong Kong's worst financial crisis in a decade has failed to dislodge its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which for the past 14 years has traded in a fixed range of around HK$7.80 to the U.S. dollar. That's because the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the former colony's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , mounted a fierce defense of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday by pushing overnight interest rates up to as high as 300%, up from around 6% the previous day. \par \par \uc2  That actually drove the Hong Kong dollar above its normal peg, prompting some monetary authorities to gloat. "Now our exchange rate is at the most strong position we've seen for a long, long time," said Hong Kong Financial Secretary Sir Donald Tsang. He and other senior officials declared that there was no reason to adjust the Hong Kong dollar's peg. \par \par \uc2  In fact, the monetary authorities have used highly unusual means to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks, raising serious questions about the territory's monetary stability. The tactic involves using government reserves as well as official foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to defend the Hong Kong dollar in the open market. \par \par \uc2  By Thursday, some hedge-fund managers had decided the tactic was a bluff and called it - expecting to win regardless of whether the U.S. dollar peg stays or falls. Some \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators, roused by recent victories against central banks around the region, redoubled their attacks on the Hong Kong dollar, claiming that it was almost in their hands. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) DOW JONES EMERGING MARKETS REPORT  24-10-97 \par \par \uc2  0311GMT \par \par \uc2  1130 1802 1805 1840 1901 62540 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020011006dtao00cjw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
