\par\par \b \uc2  Hong Kong dollar peg not like the others. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Ricci \par \uc2 728 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 1997\par 14:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Oct 23 (Reuters) - The Hong Kong dollar's link to the U.S. unit bears little resemblance to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegs that have snapped like dry twigs throughout Asia, analysts said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Unlike many of its neighbours in Asia, the Hong Kong government by law must stand ready to redeem Hong Kong dollars for U.S. dollars at a rate of HK$7.80 per US$1. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is permitted to trade in a narrow band around that level. \par \par \uc2  "There is a big difference when we talk about Hong Kong compared with Thailand, Malaysia and Indonesia," said Chi Lo, regional economist at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "Because there is a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, the HKMA (Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is not supposed to change the exchange rate policy just because of domestic economic concerns," he said. \par \par \uc2  Thailand, Malaysia and Indonesia had fixed exchange rate systems which they were eventually forced to abandon. \par \par \uc2  Central banks found that trying to maintain fixed exchange rates conflicted with monetary policy -- they would have to raise rates to support their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  even as their battered economies cried out for easier policy. \par \par \uc2  But the HKMA is bound under law to take no account of the economy when it defends the dollar. \par \par \uc2  As Hong Kong's three note issuing banks buy or sell Hong Kong dollars from the HKMA's Exchange Fund, they are bound by law to undertake a reverse transaction by selling or buying Hong Kong dollars for the Fund. \par \par \uc2  This drains, or injects, liquidity in the monetary system, so pressures for a strong Hong Kong dollar will lower interest rates and vice-versa. \par \par \uc2  The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , linked to the U.S. dollar since 1983 after a speculative attack, is basically the last pegged \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region and an obvious target, analysts have said. \par \par \uc2  Interest rates surged this week as speculators sold Hong Kong dollars and liquidity tightened. Dealers said the HKMA also was actively mopping up liquidity in the money markets. \par \par \uc2  Overnight rates rocketted to 300 percent by noon and were at 200 percent at day-end. \par \par \uc2  The surge in rates wreaked havoc on Hong Kong's stock market, with the blue-chip Hang Seng Index diving 1,211.47, or 10.41 percent, to end the day at 10,426.30. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong financial leaders said they would do nothing to stem the stock market slide and insisted that maintaining the peg was the government's main goal. \par \par \uc2  "The first priority is to defend the exchange rate. This is our top priority. We believe it is right to do so," Financial Secretary Donald Tsang told reporters on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  There is no specific mention of the dollar peg in Hong Kong's constitution, the Basic Law, which was hammered out by British and Chinese authorities in the years before the territory reverted to Chinese rule on July 1, 1997. \par \par \uc2  But the document states that the Hong Kong dollar would remain the territory's legal tender and would be allowed to circulate freely without any exchange controls. \par \par \uc2  Analysts have said that it is unthinkable that China would consider de-pegging the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because that would shake confidence in Hong Kong just a few months after the mainland assumed control. \par \par \uc2  "There is still too much at stake here both economically and politically so that Hong Kong and China officials are still quite willing to keep the peg even at a high cost in the short term," said DMG's Chi Lo. \par \par \uc2  Like many countries in Asia, Hong Kong and China have signed bilateral repurchase agreements, pledging to help each other stabilize markets in times of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong and China have combined foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of more than US$222 billion, a hefty arsenal against any attack. \par \par \uc2  But while the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the local dollar in recent months, China has not. \par \par \uc2  "Quite naturally, I think leaders in China are concerned about what is happening here, but so far, (China) has left us...to do our own work," Financial Secretary Tsang said. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong government said it dipped into its US$88.1 billion in foreign reserves most recently on Wednesday to defend the peg. \par \par \uc2  -- Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843-6371; Fax (852) 2845-0636 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtan03p5y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
