\par\par \b \uc2  HK Forwards, Interest Rates Soar As HK Dlr Confidence Fades \b0\par\par\uc2 254 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 1997\par 03:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (AP-Dow Jones)--Forward premiums in Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as well as interbank interest rates continue to climb higher in early trading Thursday, suggesting confidence in the Hong Kong dollar continues to fade, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar has strengthened early Thursday, which implies the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to prop up the tumbling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or that arbitrage players are taking advantage of steep interest rates, said the head of Treasury at a major Hong Kong bank. \par \par \uc2  The one-year premium has risen to 5,500 points Thursday from 3,000 points early Wednesday. The premium, which is added to the spot exchange rate - now hovering around HK$7.7455 to one U.S. dollar, down from HK$7.7490 Wednesday - indicates participants expect a sharply weaker Hong Kong dollar 12 months from now. \par \par \uc2  Overnight Hong Kong dollar interbank offered rates (Hibor) have shot up to 12% from 7% Wednesday, while one-week rates are up to 14% from 10% and the benchmark three-month rate has moved to 16% from 10.5%. \par \par \uc2  This week, the Hong Kong dollar has been under speculative attack, while interest rates and forward premiums have been climbing steeply higher, suggesting a broad shift in confidence in the Hong Kong dollar, which has remained comparatively steady amid the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis of the past few months. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 23-10-97 \par \par \uc2  01