\par\par \b \uc2  Hong Kong peg here to stay, but not painlessly. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Ricci \par \uc2 743 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Oct 22 (Reuters) - Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg has come under renewed scrutiny as the local dollar drifts and interest rates jump, but government officials and economists insist the peg is here to stay. \par \par \uc2  "There is no political or economic need for us to disband the Hong Kong dollar peg," the territory's Financial Secretary Donald Tsang told reporters in Hong Kong on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Speaking in London, Chief Executive Tung Chee-hwa said the Hong Kong dollar, backed by huge foreign reserves and sound fundamentals, maintained strength amid the Asian market turmoil. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar, the last major pegged \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia, is linked to the U.S. dollar at a rate of HK$7.80 per US$1, but traditionally trades on the strong side of the peg. \par \par \uc2  In recent days the spot rate has drifted to trade around HK$7.7500 per U.S. dollar from about HK$7.7375 a week ago. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's dalliance at that level caused some market jitters since HK$7.750 is widely regarded as a trigger for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but most of the actual selling was seen in the forward market. \par \par \uc2  The HKMA declined comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said that the volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar forward market was the worst since the 1994-1995 Mexico crisis, which wreaked havoc on emerging markets. \par \par \uc2  Interest rates soared on Wednesday as nervous investors sold Hong Kong dollars for U.S. dollars, damaging Hong Kong's blue chip stock index, which fell to a 13-month low of 11,637.77. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong dollars that are sold ultimately must be absorbed by the HKMA, which is pledged to maintain the local dollar's peg to the U.S. dollar, causing tighter liquidity and higher rates. \par \par \uc2  Since Asia's troubles began in July, Hong Kong periodically has been the target of speculators attempting to break the peg. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the latest round of market jitters was set off by Taiwan's decision to float its dollar. The idea was that if the Taiwan dollar could go, so too could the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  Rubbish, economists said. \par \par \uc2  "Can you see the headlines? Four months into Chinese rule and the Chinese screw up the peg. It's just not going to happen," said Andrew Freris, managing director at BA Asia. \par \par \uc2  China has vowed to retain the dollar link and the peg is enshrined in Hong Kong law. \par \par \uc2  "If China lets the peg go, there will be all sorts of imaginings. People will think that China might violate the Basic Law (Hong Kong's constitution)," said Chi Lo, regional economist at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Unlike other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia that had been pegged to the dollar or a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket, Hong Kong maintains a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, meaning that every Hong Kong dollar is backed by its equivalent in U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  DMG's Chi Lo said the HKMA could run into trouble because it has adopted the role of lender of last resort, meaning reserves could be depleted if there was a catastrophic loss of confidence and people demanded U.S. dollars all at once. \par \par \uc2  "But that potential problem has been eased by very good cooperation between the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the banks in Hong Kong, which provide the HKMA with information on what is getting done in the market and in what size," he said. \par \par \uc2  Economists and policymakers have said that concerns about Hong Kong's loss of competitiveness in a region where most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been devalued is misplaced because goods exports make up a small part of the territory's economy. \par \par \uc2  But maintaining the peg will not be without pain. \par \par \uc2  "In the process of defending the (Hong Kong) dollar, interest rates may have to go up," said Tung. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's economy is highly interest rate sensitive because so much of the economy depends on the property sector. \par \par \uc2  "The peg will stay, but the stock market will go lower, wage rates will go down and unemployment will go up," said Steven Xu, regional treasury economist at Standard Chartered Bank. \par \par \uc2  "But what else can Hong Kong do? It is a small open economy. If the peg goes, there will be so much chaos the costs will be very high," he said. \par \par \uc2  -- Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843-6371; Fax (852) 2845-0636 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtam03y2r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
