\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-HK shares battered down to six-month low. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Stephen Weeks \par \uc2 631 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Oct 21 (Reuters) - Hong Kong stocks were pounded down for a second straight day on Tuesday, closing at a six-month low, as investors bailed out on fears of higher interest rates and regional turmoil, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The blue chip Hang Seng Index, which plunged 630 points on Monday, closed at 12,403.10, down 567.78 points or 4.38 percent -- the lowest finish since April. The index had been down more than 615 points on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar was also under pressure, raising fears that the stock market rout could rattle money markets as well. \par \par \uc2  "We are in the midst of another wave of panic selling, which doesn't appear to have fully run its course," said a broker at a large British brokerage. \par \par \uc2  International analysts were putting the spotlight on Hong Kong, suggesting it could now be swept up in the turmoil which has savaged stock markets and devastated \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a number of Asian countries since July. \par \par \uc2  "I visited Asia predisposed to thinking there must be some value in the ashes of the destruction that occurred there, but I now believe a vicious circle is at work on the downside," said Morgan Stanley's chief global economist Barton Biggs. \par \par \uc2  "The second leg of the decline in Asia may have begun, in which Hong Kong could lead on the downside," he was quoted as saying in a Morgan Stanley Dean Witter equity research summary. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong selloff swept through blue chip property and industrial shares and slammed the one-time darlings of the market, the so-called red chips and H shares of China-related firms. \par \par \uc2  The stock market plunge spilled over into the Hong Kong dollar, which at one point fell through a key support level against the U.S. dollar to which it has been pegged since 1983. \par \par \uc2  The dollar weakened to HK$7.7505 in the early afternoon -- HK$7.75 is seen as a key support point, and one that could trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA). \par \par \uc2  Traders said the HKMA had offered three-month money at 8.75 percent in the morning. Three month money was quoted at 9.00 percent in early afternoon trade. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar is pegged to the U.S. dollar at a rate of US$1 for every HK$7.80, but it usually trades on the strong side of the peg. \par \par \uc2  Dollar weakness was attributed mainly to the share selloff, but also to fears the battering taken by other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months could hit Hong Kong and that speculators might move in to test the dollar peg. \par \par \uc2  "I think it's something to do with the stock market, and it's the psychological effect of the Taiwan dollar," one trader said. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar has tumbled since Friday when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  abandoned its defence around T$28.5. \par \par \uc2  China-related shares were hit hard again as well, swept up in the general flight from Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  The red chip Hong Kong China-affiliated Corporation Index dropped 257 points, or 10 percent, to 2,314 points during afternoon trade and the Hang Seng China Enterprises Index of H shares was down 62 points, or 6.62 percent, to 881. \par \par \uc2  The wave of China-related selling set a gloomy stage for the debut of China Telecom (Hong Kong) on Thursday. Shares in the China-related company were being quoted below their $HK11.80 issue price in the unofficial grey market on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  China Telecom (Hong Kong), which operates mobile phones in southern China, had been expected to meet huge success but now looks like struggling just to reach its asking price. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtal03wqn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
