\par\par \b \uc2  HK shares pounded, dollar down as investors bail out. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Stephen Weeks \par \uc2 572 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 1997\par 11:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Oct 21 (Reuters) - Hong Kong stocks were pounded down for a second day on Tuesday, shedding more than 550 points to close at a six-month low, with investors bailing out on fears of higher interest rates and regional turmoil, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The blue chip Hang Seng Index closed at 12,403.10, down 567.78 points or 4.38 percent -- the lowest finish since April. The index had been down more than 615 points on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar was also under pressure, raising fears that the stock market rout could rattle money markets as well. \par \par \uc2  "We are in the midst of another wave of panic selling, which doesn't appear to have fully run its course," said a broker at a large British house. \par \par \uc2  The selloff swept through blue chip property and industrial shares and battered the once hugely popular China-related firms -- the so-called red chips and H shares. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said investors were spooked by rising mortgage rates and regional economic turmoil which has left Hong Kong looking over-priced compared with its neighbours. \par \par \uc2  "The HIBOR's (Hong Kong interbank rate) rise to over nine percent has people worried that when banks meet Friday they'll decide to raise interest rates," said analyst Stephen Chu at Sassoon Securities. \par \par \uc2  The stock market plunge spilled over into the Hong Kong dollar, which at one point fell through a key support level against the U.S. dollar to which it has been pegged since 1983. \par \par \uc2  The dollar weakened to HK$7.7505 in the early afternoon -- HK$7.75 is seen as a key support point, and one that could trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by 