\par\par \b \uc2  Philippines: Ramos Defends  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 149 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 1997\par 05:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  (AP-Dow Jones)--Philippine President Fidel Ramos defended the recent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as a defensive move to protect the country's reserves rather than interference with free market forces. \par \par \uc2  Ramos, speaking at the World Economic Forum Tuesday in Hong Kong, said the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had abandoned its active role in propping up the peso. \par \par \uc2  'In response to the turmoil in regional markets, we decided to further open the economy and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided the peso-dollar exchange rate would be left to market forces,' Ramos said. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine president said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  periodically steps in to ensure the country meets its foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  obligations, but is not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market simply to guarantee temporary relief for the peso's stability. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 14-10-97 \par \par \uc2  0350GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dtae07nbg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
