\par\par \b \uc2  HK Fin Sec Tsang: HK Dlr Now Stable, Warns Speculators \b0\par\par\uc2 255 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 1997\par 06:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (AP-Dow Jones)--In an apparent effort to put a reassuring face on the market's recent assault on the Hong Kong dollar, Hong Kong's Financial Secretary Donald Tsang said Saturday that the Hong Kong dollar has stabilized against the U.S. dollar, and the 'so-called crisis' is basically over. \par \par \uc2  He also issued a veiled warning to speculators about the cost of assailing the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . 'I suppose speculators, having been burned, will not want to come back too quickly,' he said. 'We will always be prepared for this sort of thing.' \par \par \uc2  'There were a couple of skirmishes, and we came out all right,' said Tsang. 'We're confident about the Hong Kong economy, we're confident about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and we don't believe there will be a problem with Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .' \par \par \uc2  Tsang spoke to reporters on the way to the airport to visit Australia. He will visit three cities in Australia next week - Canberra, Sydney and Melbourne, as part of a regular visit to member countries of APEC. \par \par \uc2  'We will always be prepared for this sort of thing,' he added. \par \par \uc2  Tsang declined to say whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the open market to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the latest crisis. \par \par \uc2  In mid-July, the HKMA spent at least US$1 billion of its reserves of US$81 billion to defend the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 23-08-97 \par \par \uc2  0