\par\par \b \uc2  Experts debate fate of HK dollar on volatile day. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chris Kaufman \par \uc2 645 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 August 1997\par 14:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Aug 15 (Reuter) - While \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  money market dealers warily awaited \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the local dollar slipped lower, analysts on Friday debated the future of Asia's last major U.S. dollar fixed exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  "With what's happening on the Indonesian rupiah, there's a little bit of tension here," Andrew Fung, head of Asian Capital Markets for Commonwealth Bank of Australia in Hong Kong, told Reuters Financial Television's daily programme Asia Today. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, Indonesian officials effectively lifted the fixed exchange regime that had kept the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trade limited to a 12 percent band. \par \par \uc2  Spillover pressure from the managed flotation of the rupiah was being felt throughout Asia on Friday and was building up in Hong Kong where the local exchange rate has been fixed at HK$7.8 per US$1.00 since 1983. \par \par \uc2  Throughout the recent months of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turmoil in Asia, the Hong Kong dollar has stayed on the strong side of its unofficial one percent trading band, rarely straying from HK$7.75 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  But forward Hong Kong dollar rates have soared in response to regional pressures. \par \par \uc2  By Friday afternoon, six-month forward contracts were trading at a 1000 point premium to spot, and one-year money was at a 1500 point premium, making the carrying cost of a Hong Kong dollar short position very expensive. \par \par \uc2  In many traders' eyes, the Hong Kong dollar is starting to look very isolated in a region battered by waves of speculative pressure. \par \par \uc2  "I think there's definetely some reconsideration to be had," said Eric Nickerson, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  "The fundamentals of Hong Kong are very much Asia-oriented, not U.S. oriented," said Nickerson. "Certainly the U.S. dollar is an important \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for this economy, but to have all of its financial policy dictated by the Federal Reserve really doesn't make any sense," he said. \par \par \uc2  Chi Lo, regional economist at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell in Singapore, said Hong Kong authorities may well want to rethink the link to the U.S. dollar, but will not be forced to do so for some time, at least until the end of the century. \par \par \uc2  But Nickerson disagreed, saying the uncertainty associated with the July 1 handover of Hong Kong to China was in the past, making the political justification of the peg less viable. \par \par \uc2  While agreeing that the political uncertainty of the handover was no longer a factor for the Hong Kong dollar, Lo pointed to continued jitters over the "one country, two systems" model that permits a westernized, capitalist Hong Kong to exist inside a communist ruled China. \par \par \uc2  "Foreigners, as well as Hong Kong people, they are all skeptical," about one country, two systems, said Lo. "They need a kind of confidence booster, and the Hong Kong dollar peg will serve this purpose in the medium term." \par \par \uc2  Nickerson admitted the resolve of Hong Kong authorities to maintain the peg is strong, and that the combined financial resources of the HKMA and the Peoples Bank of China - if called upon - would total more than US$200 billion. \par \par \uc2  But he warned that the market may not care. \par \par \uc2  "That's not necessarily going to be sufficient to keep the market at bay given the uncertain circumstances in the rest of the region," said Nickerson. \par \par \uc2  CBA's Fung said that, while the market is nervous, he does not expect the Hong Kong dollar to be shoved from its U.S. dollar peg amid the current pressure. \par \par \uc2  "I think the forward will stay higher, at a signifcant premium against the U.S. interest rate, as the major disincentive for speculators to try their luck here," he said. \par \par \uc2  -- Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843-6935; Fax (852) 2845-0636 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt8f016ss\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
