\par\par \b \uc2  Hong Kong Shares Plummet 2.4% Ahead Of 3-Day Weekend \b0\par\par\uc2 634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 August 1997\par 11:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG -(Dow Jones)- Hong Kong stocks plunged 2.4% on a late Friday afternoon sell-off, prompted by worries about the stability of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and profit-taking before a long weekend. \par \par \uc2  The blue-chip Hang Seng index fell 400.83 points, or 2.4%, to close at 16096.88. It tested a low of 16002.90 and a high of 16555.51 during Friday's session after Thursday's 14.78-point gain. \par \par \uc2  Trading volume was HK$30.72 billion, up from HK$29.11 billion in the previous session. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar's overnight interbank-lending rates shot up to 7% early Friday, but the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was only marginally weaker, suggesting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the territory's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to support the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The Hong Kong dollar exchange rate isn't that stable, and people are taking profits before the long weekend," said a trader at Yamaichi International (Hong Kong). \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong stock exchange will be closed Monday because of a public holiday. \par \par \uc2  Equity traders said Friday's market decline was led by interest rate-sensitive banking and property sectors, as investors were worried about an increase in the prime interest rates, driven up by higher money-market rates. \par \par \uc2  On Wall Street, the Dow Jones Industrial Average added 13.71 points to 7942.03 in a second-straight day of volatile trading. U.S. equities got a lift from the bond market; the benchmark 30-year bond rose 28/32 in price, as its yield fell to 6.55%. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong investors focus on the U.S. economy because the territory's monetary policy closely tracks U.S. interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the broader All Ordinaries index was down 165.28 points at 8254.38. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Property subindex dropped 875.56 points, or 3.1%, to end at 26940.82. The Hang Seng Financial subindex also lost 424.54 points, or 2.1%, to close at 20661.85. \par \par \uc2  Cheung Kong (Holdings) fell HK$2.25 or 2.4%, to close at HK$92. Sung Hung Kai Properties also dropped HK$3, or 3%, to HK$96.25. Henderson Land Development fell HK$2.75, or 3.8%, to HK$70.25. \par \par \uc2  British bank HSBC Holdings PLC lost HK$5, or 1.8%, to HK$272. Hang Seng Bank slid HK$3, or 2.8%, to HK$104.5. \par \par \uc2  China-backed conglomerate CITIC Pacific topped the most-actives list. The blue-chip share lost HK$3, or 5.9%, closing at HK$48 amid heavy trading valued at HK$1.23 billion. \par \par \uc2  Ka Wah Bank was also active, bucking the market downtrend to gain HK$1, or 7.6%, ending at HK$14.15. The medium-sized local bank already jumped 21% Thursday on news that its controlling share holder, Beijing-based conglomerate China International Trust & Investment, plans to delist Ka Wah Bank from Hong Kong's stock exchange and replace the counter with a new holding company which includes Ka Wah Bank and CITIC's own Industrial Bank. \par \par \uc2  The Beijing-based CITIC is also the parent company for the Hong Kong-listed CITIC Pacific. An analyst with a European brokerage said CITIC Pacific shares slipped Friday as some investors believed that the Beijing parent is more likely to inject valuable assets into the new holding company instead of CITIC Pacific. \par \par \uc2  H shares, or China's state-owned enterprises listed in the territory, were hit by profit-taking after soaring in the past couple of weeks. The Hang Seng China Enterprises index, which tracks H shares, lost 6.2%, or 93.32 points, to 1416.92. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1997 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020011006dt8f00o3k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
