\par\par \b \uc2  HK spent US$1.0 bln defending HK dollar in July. \b0\par\par\uc2 341 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Aug 12 (Reuter) - The Hong Kong government spent US$1.0 billion from its reserves fending off speculative attacks against the Hong Kong dollar in July, a government spokeswoman said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "Everything was resolved in a very swift manner," she said. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong government radio earlier quoted Financial Secretary Donald Tsang as saying that the government had repelled the attack against the Hong Kong dollar in about two hours on an unspecified day in July, spending US$1.0 billion. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar has been pegged at HK$7.8 to the U.S. dollar since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunged in 1983 due to worries over the future of the former British colony, which returned to China in June this year. \par \par \uc2  Several Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been targeted by speculators over the last several weeks in the wake of Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  The spokeswoman said the government also knew the identity of the speculator, but said Tsang had declined to identify the attacker. \par \par \uc2  Another government official, who declined to be named, told Reuters that whatever the authorities might have spent on defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not lost because the government could reverse its position in the market later once \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility had eased. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  held up well on Tuesday despite heavy selling of other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , notably the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The Hong Kong dollar was little changed despite the drop in the Singapore dollar and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though there was some movement in the forward rates," said one dealer. \par \par \uc2  "The market is very concerned about the HKMA (Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) coming in because they are very serious (about defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 )," he said. \par \par \uc2  But there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Tuesday's session, the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar was trading at HK$7.7433/43 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  --Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843-6346; Fax (852) 2845-0636 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt8c01eba\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
