\par\par \b \uc2  HK Used/Defending HK Dollar -2: Second Half Of July \b0\par\par\uc2 217 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 1997\par 12:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HK's Financial Secretary Donald Tsang reportedly made the comments after reporters asked him about the day-earlier announcement that Hong Kong would participate in the International Monetary Fund-sponsored bailout of Thailand. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong is making US$1.0 billion available to the Bank of Thailand, through the aegis of the IMF. Tsang on Monday said that forwarding money to the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would help stabilize the baht. A more stable baht would reduce the likelihood of spillover \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling elsewhere in the region, and weakening of the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  The official didn't say exactly when the speculative activity took place. But in the second half of July, Hong Kong interbank offered rates were pushed extra high for almost a week, as forward exchange rates for the Hong Kong dollar suggested the market expected \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would weaken later. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market on more than one day that week, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The HKMA, the quasi \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is charged with defending the peg that values US$1.0 at HK$7.8000. It also holds the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which at mid-year were worth HK$67.9 billion. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 12-08-97 \par \par \uc2  1057GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt8c05wp6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
