\par\par \b \uc2  Speculators position for HK dollar attack - traders. \b0\par\par\uc2 615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juli 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, July 24 (Reuter) - Hong Kong money market traders said fund managers around the world were preparing to attack the Hong Kong dollar, buying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards aggressively. \par \par \uc2  "It's the usual case where people want to speculate against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and they have to fund themselves first," said one head \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a local bank. "Fund managers around the world will be trying to attack the Hong Kong dollar and they are trying to buy forwards." \par \par \uc2  Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards reached their highest levels since the 1995 speculative attack against the Hong Kong dollar, which followed the Mexican peso crisis. \par \par \uc2  Three-month \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fowards reached 350 points while the six-month soared to 550 from Wednesday's levels of 130 and 170 respectively. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar spot rate remained steady at HK$7.7428/38. The unit is pegged at HK$7.80 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment on market conditions, or confirm its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We will closely monitor the situation," said HKMA spokesman Thoman Chan. "I've just come back from the dealing rooms, I know what is happening in the market, I know what traders have said to reporters. The HKMA will monitor the market and we don't comment on money market rumours." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said funds were buying U.S. dollar foward, anticipating a fall in the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  "It's speculation and the spillover from speculation," said Callum Henderson at MMS International. "Previously banks wouldn't necessarily hedge their short U.S. dollar exposure." \par \par \uc2  Customers bailing out of Hong Kong dollars are leaving Hong Kong banks short of U.S. dollars short-term. Banks are now going to the market to make sure they are not exposed to the Hong Kong dollar, Henderson said, where previously they would not bother. \par \par \uc2  Although the HKMA declined to comment, traders said the HKMA was mopping up liquidity in the short end, keeping conditions tight by buying up all the excess Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  One dealer reported the HKMA selling one-month Hong Kong dollar Exchange Fund bills at 7.71 percent -- far above the one-month market yield of 6.56 percent. \par \par \uc2  "He (HKMA chief Joseph Yam) hit the market twice. We expect short-dated money will squeeze up dramatically on Friday," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Two initial public offerings were due to be released on Friday, which would tighten liquidity further in Hong Kong's small and tightly-traded money market. \par \par \uc2  The overnight shot above the key level of seven percent this morning before settling back to 6.75 percent after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they were very nervous that rates would soar sky-high on Friday, forcing them to bail out of positions. \par \par \uc2  "We will take losses. The mood, in one word, is very frightened," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "(The spot rate) didn't do what (Yam) wanted it to after he \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday. It still went back to HK$7.75 and this made him crazy and angry. Tomorrow is a good day, he can do anything. He can squeeze rates up to 55 percent if he wants." \par \par \uc2  But some players said the activity was driven more by "prudent hedging" than speculation. Substantial scepticism about the likelihood of a successful attack against the Hong Kong dollar remained much in evidence as the morning session closed. \par \par \uc2  The HKMA holds at least US$63 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, and has a commitment of support from China, which holds in excess of US$112 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  -- Sarah Davison (852) 2843 6470 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt7o0244d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
